{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent level bias: model forecasts are generally too low vs. realized prices in the first ~1–15 days (often by ~0.2–1.5 €/t). Apply an upward intercept adjustment early-horizon, tapering toward zero by ~day 20.",
    "Over-optimism at longer horizons: beyond ~day 18–30 the model increasingly overshoots (notably in Examples 4–5, ending ~+1–2 €/t). Apply a downward drift/tilt to the forecast path after ~day 15–20.",
    "Excess volatility / overshoot around peaks: model tends to exaggerate post-run-up strength (predicts higher highs and sustained elevation), while realized series mean-reverts sooner. Damp forecast deviations from the recent mean (e.g., shrink toward 10–20d average) when the model projects continued acceleration after a spike.",
    "Turning-point lag: model is slow to reflect reversals; it keeps projecting strength into periods where outcomes flatten or soften. Introduce a reversal check: if recent momentum is positive but realized volatility is high, cap forward gains and increase mean-reversion weight.",
    "Horizon-shape correction: replace a near-linear upward continuation with a hump-shaped path (rise then mild fade) when the last 5–10 days show a sharp upswing; this matches observed quicker normalization in outcomes."
  ]
}
